<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sallust</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/86-bce">86</a>–c. 35 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Gaius Sallustius Crispus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sallust (Gaius Sallustius Crispus) was a Roman politician and historian. A partisan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, he became the first governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa-nova">Africa Nova</a>, the former Numidian kingdom, after 46 BCE and was accused of enriching himself there. In retirement he wrote The Conspiracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catiline">Catiline</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellum-iugurthinum">The Jugurthine War</a>, a major source for Numidia and the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, and a History of which fragments survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sallust.jpg" alt="Sallust" width="320" height="576" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philippe Trière d'après «Maréchal» · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salluste_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Horses had been the pride of the Armenian warrior since ancient times, and <b>Sallust</b> called the cavalry 'remarkable by the beauty of their horses and armor'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The historian <b>Sallust</b>, whose Jugurthine War is the main account of Numidia, was the first governor of the Roman province that replaced the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Contact with the Greek world transformed Roman culture: Livius Andronicus translated the Odyssey into Latin around 240 BCE, Plautus and Terence adapted Greek comedy, Ennius wrote the epic Annales, and in the late Republic Cicero, Lucretius, Catullus, <b>Sallust</b> and Caesar created a classical Latin prose and poetry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman authors such as <b>Sallust</b> mention books in Punic, but none survive except in translation, as Mago’s treatise does, or in snippets, as in the plays of Plautus, whose Poenulus gives Punic words in Latin spelling, such as chyl, ‘all’.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gaetuli are known from Roman writers such as <b>Sallust</b> and Strabo, who used the name for the nomadic and semi-nomadic Berber peoples living along the Saharan Atlas, from southern Morocco to southern Tunisia.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaetuli">Gaetuli</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiternum">Amiternum</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.4,13.31&amp;year=-53" aria-label="Amiternum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4873 656 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sallust"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5323 926h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5323 926h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.4,13.31&amp;year=-53">What was here in 53 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Without reason do mankind complain of their nature, on the ground that it is weak and of short duration and ruled rather by chance than by virtue.&#8221;</p><footer>The Jugurthine War, 1 &#183; tr. J. C. Rolfe, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Sallust/Bellum_Jugurthinum/1*.html" rel="noopener">penelope.uchicago.edu</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Accordingly, good morals were cultivated at home and in the field; there was the greatest harmony and little or no avarice; justice and probity prevailed among them, thanks not so much to laws as to nature.&#8221;</p><footer>The War with Catiline, 9 &#183; tr. J. C. Rolfe, 1921 &#183; of the early Roman Republic &#183; <a href="https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Sallust/Bellum_Catilinae*.html" rel="noopener">penelope.uchicago.edu</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Therefore as the result of riches, luxury and greed, united with insolence, took possession of our young manhood.&#8221;</p><footer>The War with Catiline, 12 &#183; tr. J. C. Rolfe, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Sallust/Bellum_Catilinae*.html" rel="noopener">penelope.uchicago.edu</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
